---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/asbe/asbe04.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "1. Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "2. Scope and Recognition Criteria" },
        { id: "s3", label: "3. Initial Measurement — At Cost" },
        { id: "s4", label: "4. THE KEY DIVERGENCE — No Revaluation Model Permitted" },
        { id: "s5", label: "5. Componentisation" },
        { id: "s6", label: "6. Depreciation Methods and Useful Life" },
        { id: "s7", label: "7. Subsequent Expenditure" },
        { id: "s8", label: "8. Worked Example — Componentised Depreciation" },
        { id: "s9", label: "9. Worked Example — Capitalising a Major Overhaul" },
        { id: "s10", label: "10. Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s11", label: "11. Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s12", label: "12. Relationship to IAS 16" },
        { id: "s13", label: "13. Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s14", label: "14. Related Standards" },
        { id: "s15", label: "15. Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">
</div>



<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Tangible assets used over more than one period</div><div class="principle">ASBE No. 4 governs the RECOGNITION, MEASUREMENT, and DISCLOSURE of FIXED ASSETS — TANGIBLE assets HELD for use in the PRODUCTION or supply of GOODS or SERVICES, for RENTAL to OTHERS, or for ADMINISTRATIVE purposes, EXPECTED to be used for MORE than ONE accounting PERIOD. The STANDARD broadly PARALLELS IAS 16 internationally, with ONE genuinely SIGNIFICANT measurement divergence (Section 4).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope and Recognition Criteria</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Probable future economic benefits, reliably measurable cost</div><div class="principle">A FIXED ASSET is RECOGNISED where it is PROBABLE that FUTURE economic BENEFITS associated with the ASSET will FLOW to the ENTERPRISE, and the ASSET's cost can be MEASURED reliably — CONSISTENT with the GENERAL asset recognition PRINCIPLES applied THROUGHOUT the ASBE suite.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Initial Measurement — At Cost</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Purchase price, taxes, and costs to bring the asset to working condition</div><div class="text-supporting">A FIXED ASSET is INITIALLY measured at COST — the PURCHASE price, RELATED taxes, and OTHER costs DIRECTLY attributable to BRINGING the asset to its INTENDED working condition and LOCATION (e.g. TRANSPORT, installation, and TESTING costs).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>THE KEY DIVERGENCE — No Revaluation Model Permitted</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cost model only, throughout an asset's life</div><div class="text-supporting">UNLIKE IAS 16, which permits an ENTITY to ELECT the REVALUATION model (CARRYING an asset CLASS at FAIR VALUE, updated REGULARLY, WITH increases GENERALLY recognised in OTHER comprehensive income) as an ACCOUNTING policy CHOICE, ASBE No. 4 permits ONLY the COST model — fixed ASSETS are CARRIED at COST less ACCUMULATED depreciation and IMPAIRMENT, WITH NO REVALUATION option AVAILABLE. This is a GENUINELY significant, WELL-KNOWN measurement DIVERGENCE, CONSISTENT with the SAME general CAUTION around FAIR VALUE MEASUREMENT seen ELSEWHERE across the ASBE ASSET standards (Investment Property, BIOLOGICAL Assets — see the COMPANION posters).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Componentisation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Separate depreciation for components with materially different useful lives</div><div class="text-supporting">WHERE a FIXED asset COMPRISES SEPARATE, SIGNIFICANT components with MATERIALLY DIFFERENT useful LIVES (e.g. an AIRCRAFT'S engines VERSUS its AIRFRAME), EACH component is DEPRECIATED SEPARATELY over its OWN useful LIFE — CONSISTENT with IAS 16's OWN componentisation REQUIREMENT internationally.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Depreciation Methods and Useful Life</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A systematic allocation reflecting the pattern of consumption</div><div class="text-supporting">DEPRECIATION is CALCULATED using a METHOD reflecting the PATTERN in which the ASSET'S future ECONOMIC benefits are EXPECTED to be CONSUMED — STRAIGHT-LINE, units-of-PRODUCTION, or an ACCELERATED method (e.g. DOUBLE-declining BALANCE) — over the ASSET'S estimated USEFUL life, DOWN to its ESTIMATED residual VALUE.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Subsequent Expenditure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Capitalised only if recognition criteria are met</div><div class="text-supporting">SUBSEQUENT expenditure on a FIXED asset (e.g. a MAJOR overhaul or UPGRADE) is CAPITALISED ONLY where it MEETS the GENERAL recognition CRITERIA — PROBABLE future ECONOMIC benefits and RELIABLE cost MEASUREMENT — OTHERWISE, it is EXPENSED as INCURRED, CONSISTENT with ROUTINE repairs and MAINTENANCE costs.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — Componentised Depreciation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Separate useful lives for significant components</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A manufacturing machine with a replaceable core component</div><div class="wk-body">A company purchases a machine for RMB 10,000,000, comprising a core processing unit costing RMB 4,000,000 with a 5-year useful life, and the remaining structure costing RMB 6,000,000 with a 15-year useful life.</div><div class="wk-answer">The company depreciates the CORE unit separately: RMB 4,000,000 ÷ 5 = <strong>RMB 800,000 per year</strong>, and the REMAINING structure separately: RMB 6,000,000 ÷ 15 = <strong>RMB 400,000 per year</strong> — a TOTAL annual depreciation of RMB 1,200,000, REFLECTING each COMPONENT'S own distinct CONSUMPTION pattern.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Capitalising a Major Overhaul</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Distinguishing capital expenditure from routine repairs</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A major equipment overhaul extending useful life</div><div class="wk-body">A company spends RMB 1,500,000 on a major overhaul of production equipment, extending its remaining useful life by 5 years and enhancing its output capacity.</div><div class="wk-answer">Since the overhaul PROVIDES incremental FUTURE economic BENEFITS (an EXTENDED useful LIFE and ENHANCED capacity), the RMB 1,500,000 is <strong>CAPITALISED</strong> and DEPRECIATED over the REVISED remaining useful LIFE — RATHER than EXPENSED immediately as a ROUTINE repair would be.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASBE No. 4 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Depreciation methods and useful lives, by class of fixed asset</span><span>Gross carrying amount and accumulated depreciation, at the beginning and end of the period</span><span>Fixed assets pledged as security, and those temporarily idle</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Identifying significant components requiring separate depreciation</span><span>Whether subsequent expenditure meets the capitalisation criteria</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Useful life, residual value, and depreciation method</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Relationship to IAS 16</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASBE 4 vs IAS 16</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASBE 4</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 16</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Measurement model</span><span>COST model only</span><span>Cost model OR revaluation model, as a policy choice</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Componentisation</span><span>Required</span><span>Required — converged</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">The ABSENCE of a revaluation OPTION is a GENUINELY significant, RECURRING theme ACROSS the ASBE ASSET standards — CONSISTENTLY prioritising HISTORICAL cost's VERIFIABILITY over FAIR VALUE'S potential RELEVANCE.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every fixed asset event lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Depreciation</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised as an expense (or capitalised into inventory/other assets, where relevant)</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASBE No. 5</span><span>Biological Assets — governs living animals and plants, which follow a similar cost-model-based approach but sit outside this Standard's own scope</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASBE No. 8</span><span>Impairment of Assets — governs the no-reversal impairment framework applicable to fixed assets</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Componentisation</div><div class="glossary-def">Depreciating significant parts of a fixed asset separately when they have materially different useful lives from the asset as a whole.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Residual value</div><div class="glossary-def">The estimated amount an enterprise would currently obtain from disposal of an asset, after deducting estimated disposal costs, at the end of its useful life.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Chinese GAAP / ASBE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><div class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet (资产负债表) </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge bp">IS</span> Income Statement (利润表) </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge bo">SCOE</span> Statement of Changes in Owners' Equity (所有者权益变动表) </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge bc">CFS</span> Cash Flow Statement (现金流量表)</div></div></div>


</div>
  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asbe04 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
